<content>
  <section><title>Solve Applications of Systems of Inequalities</title>
<para>When we use variables other than \(x\) and \(y\) to define an unknown quantity, we must change the names of the axes of the graph as well.</para>
<para><emphasis>Example</emphasis></para>
<blockquote>
  <para>Omar needs to eat at least 800 calories before going to his team practice. All he wants is hamburgers, \(h\), and cookies, \(c\), and he doesn’t want to spend more than $5. At the hamburger restaurant near his college, each hamburger has 240 calories and costs $1.40. Each cookie has 160 calories and costs $0.50.</para>
</blockquote>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>Write a system of inequalities to model this situation.<emphasis>Solution</emphasis></item>
  <list type="none">
    <item>The calories from hamburgers at 240 calories each, plus the calories from cookies at 160 calories each must be 800 or more.</item><item>The amount spent on hamburgers at $1.40 each, plus the amount spent on cookies at $0.50 each must be no more than $5.00.</item><item>The number of hamburgers must be greater than or equal to 0.</item>
    <item>We have our system of equations.</item>
  </list>
  <item>Graph the system.<emphasis>Solution</emphasis><list type="none">
      <item>To graph \(240h+160c≥80\), graph \(240h+160c=800\) as a solid line. Choose \((0,0)\) as a test point. Since it does not make the inequality true, shade (red) the side that does not include the point \((0,0)\).<media><image alt="graph of 240h+160c is greater than or equal to 800 and graph of 1.40h +0.50c is less than or equal to 5" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/a33241cd08b3ea9848101b3823e7dcf56f94a8ac"/>
      </media></item>
      <item>Graph \(1.40h+0.50c≤5\).</item>
    </list>
  </item> <item>Could he eat 3 hamburgers and 1 cookie?<emphasis>Solution</emphasis></item><item>Could he eat 2 hamburgers and 4 cookies?<emphasis>Solution</emphasis></item>
</list>
<title>Try It: Solve Applications of Systems of Inequalities</title>
<para>Use the following information for questions 1-4.</para><blockquote>Philip’s doctor tells him he should add at least 1,000 more calories per day to his usual diet. Philip wants to buy protein bars, \(p\), that cost $1.80 each and have 140 calories and juice, \(j\), that costs $1.25 per bottle and has 125 calories. He doesn’t want to spend more than $12.</blockquote>

<!-- BEGIN TEXT ENTRY WITH REVEAL -->
<!--Q#1-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Write a system of inequalities that models this situation.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para> \(\left\{\begin{array}{l}140p+125j\geq1000\\1.80p+1.25j\leq12\end{array}\right.\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->
<!-- END TEXT ENTRY WITH REVEAL -->
